\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% TRANSFORMATION TNOControlADJP      (* CLOSEDADJPPROPs *)
< m1: CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
            [ I3:mu1,
              predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                     [ I1:mu2,
                       complrel/CLOSEDADJPPROP{CLOSEDADJPPROPrec1}
                              [ I2:subjrel/T1,
                                predrel/ADJP{ADJPrec1}[mu3]
                              ],
                       mu4
                     ],
              mu5 
            ]
>

< m : CLAUSE{CLAUSErec2}
            [ I3:mu1,
              predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                     [ I1:mu2,
                       I2:objrel/T1,
                       predrel/ADJP{ADJPrec1}[mu3],
                       mu4
                     ],
              mu5 
            ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: NOT EXIST (mu2, [objrel/..])
       I2: T1.CAT IN [NP, NPVAR, CNVAR]
       I3: NOT EXIST (mu1, [I31:auxrel/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu11]])
         I31: VERBrec1.subc = modalverb
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: CLOSEDADJPPROPrec1 = COPYT_adjpTOadjpprop(ADJPrec1) 
                 EXCEPT FOR [aktionsarts, superdeixis, PROsubject] 
              AND CLOSEDADJPPROPrec1 = { aktionsarts   : [stative ]
                                         superdeixis   : omegadeixis
                                         PROsubject    : false } AND
              CLAUSErec1.synvpefs * AUX_closedxppvps <> []
          A1: CLAUSErec2 := CLAUSErec1
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: CLAUSErec2.synvpefs * AUX_closedxppvps <> []
          A1: CLOSEDADJPPROPrec1 := COPYT_adjpTOadjpprop(ADJPrec1);
              CLOSEDADJPPROPrec1 := { aktionsarts   : [stative ]
                                      superdeixis   : omegadeixis
                                      PROsubject    : false };
              CLAUSErec1 := CLAUSErec2;
              CLAUSErec1.synvpefs := 
                  CLAUSErec2.synvpefs * AUX_closedxppvps
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TNOControlADJP
\item[* relevant examples:] \mbox{}\\
do turn out [it nice and sunny] $\rightarrow$ do turn out it [nice and sunny]\\
x1 consider [he foolish] $\rightarrow$ x1 consider he [foolish]\\
seem [he ill] to x2 $\rightarrow$ seem he [ill] to me\\
seem [it unlikely that he has left] $\rightarrow$ seem it [unlikely that he has 
left]
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) TC\_ControlRules
\item[* date of creation:] 23-FEB-1988
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch TNoControl1
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 01/06/90\ \ \ \ \ author:Petra de Wit
replaced specific patterns by set of CLOSED verbpatterns
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to interpret the subject of a (closed) embedded sentence as 
the object of the higher sentence. This object may be made subject of the 
higher sentence later on.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
The conditions on req and env have been removed. They were:\\
If T1 is an NP, then the req of ADJPPROP equals the most 'dominant' one of
NPrec1.req and ADJPrec1.req (where pospol,negpol 'more dominant' than 
[pospol, negpol, Omegapol]). If no distinction in dominancy can be made, the 
two relevant values must be the same.\\
If T1 is an NPVAR or a CNVAR, the req of these constituents does not 
codetermine the value of the req attribute of the CLOSEDADJPPROP. 
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\
It is assumed that in the CLOSEDADJPPROP extraposition (to some place within 
the ADJP) of a subject sentence has already taken place, together with 
insertion of the dummy subject `it'. Other subjects than NP/CN(VAR)s are not 
supposed to occur, nor are empty subject positions accepted (for ergative 
adjectives).
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\

\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

